is that even if the power is switched off, the contents still remain in the device (persistence). The other advantage is that it also doubles up as a storage device and there is no need to actually pay the penalty of page faults when a new process starts or the system boots up. Given the increasing use of nonvolatile memory in laptops, desktops and serverclass processors, it was incumbent upon Linux developers to create a device-specific zone. 
